---
import Layout from "../../Base.astro";
import SimpleHero from "../../components/SimpleHero.astro";
import FeaturedArticles from "../../components/FeaturedArticles.astro";
import ServicesRelatedWork from "../../components/ServicesRelatedWork.astro";
import "../../styles/pages/_services-list.scss";
---

<Layout
  title="Branded Apparel Design | Kelp Agency"
  description={`When your brand has fans but nothing wearable they actually want to keep, apparel becomes wasted spend. We design merch people want to wear and businesses can confidently produce.`}
>
  <SimpleHero
    tagline="Branding & Design Services"
    title="Apparel Design"
    description="When your brand has fans but nothing wearable they actually want to keep, apparel becomes wasted spend. We design merch people want to wear and businesses can confidently produce."
    lottieFile="apparel-design.json"
  />
  <div class="service-body">
    <div class="wrapper wrapper--narrow">
      <h2>Where teams get stuck</h2>
      <p>
        Apparel usually gets treated like an easy add-on: print a shirt, hand
        out a hat, make a patch. But when the design is generic or disconnected
        from the brand, the pieces do not get worn, shared, or remembered.
      </p>
      <p>
        That turns merchandise into extra inventory instead of a useful brand
        touchpoint.
      </p>
      <h2>How we fix it</h2>
      <p>
        We design apparel around the real use case, the audience, and the
        production method so the concept holds up on the actual garment, not
        just on a screen.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Patches</li>
        <li>Hats</li>
        <li>T-Shirts</li>
        <li>Outerwear</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Design for the Garment and the Wearer</h2>
      <p>
        We consider placement, scale, color, decoration method, and how the
        design reads on the actual garment. A team uniform, retail item, event
        shirt, and promotional piece each need a different balance of brand
        presence and wearability.
      </p>
      <h2>Keep the System Consistent</h2>
      <p>
        Apparel can connect to packaging, ecommerce, campaigns, and events. Our
        goal is to make those pieces feel related without repeating the same
        artwork everywhere. See apparel and brand work in Hart's Meat Market and
        Marker 48 Brewing. Tell us what the apparel needs to do.
      </p>
      <h2>How This Works in Practice</h2>
      <p>
        Apparel must work as an object someone wants to wear, not only as a logo
        placement. We consider audience, garment, print area, color, production
        method, and how the design reads from a distance. A strong apparel
        system can support an event, team, or retail line while still feeling
        connected to the broader brand. Production-ready files and clear
        specifications make the concept usable.
      </p>
      <h2>What the Work Includes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Concepts for merch, uniforms, or events</li>
        <li>Placement and color variations</li>
        <li>Production-aware art files</li>
        <li>A consistent set of designs across garments</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What Moving Forward Looks Like</h2>
      <p>
        Tell us who will wear the apparel, how it will be distributed, and what
        production method is planned. We can then develop the concept and
        technical artwork together.
      </p>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>Can you adapt an existing identity?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. We can work with current brand assets and design apparel that feels
        like a natural extension of them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/contact/" class="button button--green">
          Talk Through Your Apparel Project
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <ServicesRelatedWork tag={67} />
  </div>
  <FeaturedArticles />
</Layout>
